time="2025-12-02T00:05:45+08:00" level=info msg="message[hello_world]response[Hello there!\n\n\"Hello, World!\" is often the very first program beginners write when learning a new programming language. It's a simple way to confirm that your development environment is set up correctly and you can execute code.\n\nHere's how you'd typically write it in Python, one of the most popular languages:\n\n```python\nprint(\"Hello, World!\")\n```\n\n**Explanation:**\n\n*   `print()` is a built-in Python function that outputs whatever you pass to it to the console (your screen).\n*   `\"Hello, World!\"` is a string (a sequence of characters) that is passed as an argument to the `print()` function.\n\nWhen you run this code, it will simply display:\n\n```\nHello, World!\n```\n\nWould you like to see \"Hello, World!\" in another programming language, like Java, C++, JavaScript, or something else?]" func="github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger.(*LogrusLogger).Notice" file="/Users/sweetie/pengbaojiang/code/gosrc/gopath/src/github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger/logruslogger.go:32"
time="2025-12-02T00:06:24+08:00" level=info msg="message[hello_world]response[Hello, world! It's a classic for a reason. 😊]" func="github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger.(*LogrusLogger).Notice" file="/Users/sweetie/pengbaojiang/code/gosrc/gopath/src/github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger/logruslogger.go:32"
time="2025-12-02T00:06:48+08:00" level=info msg="message[what is hello_world]response[\"Hello, World!\" is arguably the most famous and fundamental program in the world of computer programming.\n\nHere's a breakdown of what it is:\n\n1.  **A Simple Program:** It's a very basic program whose sole function is to display the phrase \"Hello, World!\" (or a slight variation like \"hello world\") on a screen, console, or other output device.\n\n2.  **The Traditional First Program:** When learning a new programming language, writing a \"Hello, World!\" program is almost universally the very first task. It's a rite of passage for every programmer.\n\n3.  **Why is it so important?**\n    *   **Introduction to Syntax:** It's the simplest way to see the basic structure and syntax required to write *any* executable code in a given language.\n    *   **Verifying Your Environment:** If you can successfully compile and run a \"Hello, World!\" program, it means your compiler, interpreter, IDE (Integrated Development Environment), and other development tools are correctly installed and configured. This is crucial for beginners who often struggle with setup.\n    *   **Minimal Example of Output:** It demonstrates the most basic way to get a program to interact with the user (by displaying text).\n    *   **Building Confidence:** Successfully running your first program, no matter how simple, is a huge confidence booster for new learners.\n\n4.  **Examples in Different Languages:**\n    The exact code varies depending on the programming language:\n\n    *   **Python:**\n        ```python\n        print(\"Hello, World!\")\n        ```\n\n    *   **Java:**\n        ```java\n        public class HelloWorld {\n            public static void main(String[] args) {\n                System.out.println(\"Hello, World!\");\n            }\n        }\n        ```\n\n    *   **C:**\n        ```c\n        #include <stdio.h>\n\n        int main() {\n            printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\");\n            return 0;\n        }\n        ```\n\n    *   **JavaScript (in a browser console or Node.js):**\n        ```javascript\n        console.log(\"Hello, World!\");\n        ```\n\n    *   **C++:**\n        ```cpp\n        #include <iostream>\n\n        int main() {\n            std::cout << \"Hello, World!\" << std::endl;\n            return 0;\n        }\n        ```\n\n5.  **Historical Context:** The phrase \"Hello, World!\" gained widespread popularity as the example program in Brian Kernighan and Dennis Ritchie's seminal book \"The C Programming Language\" (1978). Before that, similar simple test programs existed, often printing \"hello\" or \"hi!\".\n\nIn essence, \"Hello, World!\" is the programming equivalent of taking your first step or speaking your first word in a new language. It confirms everything is working and sets the stage for more complex learning.]" func="github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger.(*LogrusLogger).Notice" file="/Users/sweetie/pengbaojiang/code/gosrc/gopath/src/github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger/logruslogger.go:32"
time="2025-12-02T00:18:56+08:00" level=info msg="message[hello world]response[Hello, world! I'm here. How can I help you today?]" func="github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger.(*LogrusLogger).Notice" file="/Users/sweetie/pengbaojiang/code/gosrc/gopath/src/github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger/logruslogger.go:32"
time="2026-01-02T01:11:19+08:00" level=info msg="message[what is hello_world]response[\"Hello, World!\" is a term deeply ingrained in the world of computer programming. It refers to a **very simple computer program that outputs or displays the text \"Hello, World!\" (or a slight variation) on a screen or console.**\n\nIt's much more than just a string of text; it holds significant meaning for several reasons:\n\n1.  **The First Program:** For almost every programmer learning a new language, \"Hello, World!\" is the very first program they write. It's a traditional \"rite of passage\" or \"baptism by fire.\"\n2.  **Verifying Setup:** Writing and successfully running \"Hello, World!\" confirms that your development environment (compiler, interpreter, IDE, etc.) is correctly installed and configured. If you can get \"Hello, World!\" to work, you know the basic tools are functional.\n3.  **Introducing Basic Syntax:** It demonstrates the most fundamental syntax for outputting text in a given programming language. It teaches you how to make the program *do* something visible.\n4.  **Simplicity:** It's the simplest possible functional program. It requires minimal code and concepts, allowing beginners to achieve a quick win without getting bogged down in complex logic.\n5.  **Historical Tradition:** The \"Hello, World!\" program gained widespread popularity from the 1978 book \"The C Programming Language\" by Brian Kernighan and Dennis Ritchie. While earlier examples exist, their book cemented it as a standard introduction.\n\n**Examples of \"Hello, World!\" in different programming languages:**\n\n*   **Python:**\n    ```python\n    print(\"Hello, World!\")\n    ```\n\n*   **JavaScript (for web browsers or Node.js):**\n    ```javascript\n    console.log(\"Hello, World!\");\n    ```\n\n*   **Java:**\n    ```java\n    public class HelloWorld {\n        public static void main(String[] args) {\n            System.out.println(\"Hello, World!\");\n        }\n    }\n    ```\n\n*   **C:**\n    ```c\n    #include <stdio.h>\n\n    int main() {\n        printf(\"Hello, World!\\n\");\n        return 0;\n    }\n    ```\n\nIn essence, \"Hello, World!\" is the universal starting point for anyone embarking on their programming journey, a simple test of an environment, and a piece of programming tradition.]" func="github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger.(*LogrusLogger).Notice" file="/Users/sweetie/pengbaojiang/code/gosrc/gopath/src/github.com/glstr/futty_golang/logger/logruslogger.go:32"
